{"role":"assistant","content":"Done. The full diagnosis is in `ANSWER.md`; here's the short version.\n\n## Why it never reported anything\n\nThe job was firing all along — `launchctl print` shows `runs = 6`, `last exit code = 1`. Two stacked bugs turned every failure into silence:\n\n1. **The agent call failed every night.** The plist's `EnvironmentVariables` only sets `PATH`, so under launchd `claude` couldn't find its keychain login and died with `Not logged in · Please run /login` (all 6 lines of `nightly-review.err`).\n2. **The failure reporter was killed before it could speak.** `bin/nightly-review.zsh:19` does `status=$?` — and `status` is a read-only special variable in zsh, so the assignment aborts the script on the spot. That line sits between the agent call and the notification, so the \"nightly review FAILED\" message never ran. I confirmed both the zsh kill behavior and the exact silent-failure sequence with mocked end-to-end runs.\n\n## Fixes\n\n- `bin/nightly-review.zsh` — `rc=$?` instead of `status=$?`; prompt piped via stdin instead of a multi-line `-p` argument; per-run outcome + duration logged.\n- `Library/LaunchAgents/com.vk.nightly-review.plist` — added `USER` = `vk` to `EnvironmentVariables`.\n\nVerified with a sandboxed fake `$HOME` and mocked agent/notifier (no real Telegram calls): failure path now notifies and exits 1, success path notifies and writes a fresh review, repo-missing path notifies, prompt arrives intact on stdin, and `zsh -n` / `plutil -lint` pass.\n\nOne thing I couldn't do from a snapshot: a real `kickstart` and real Telegram delivery. `ANSWER.md` has the `bootout`/`bootstrap`/`kickstart -k` commands to run on the real machine, plus one residual risk worth watching during that test — `notify.sh` itself runs under `set -eu` with a `security` keychain lookup, so if that ever fails under launchd it would also die silently."}
